ology can really help someone even  if it's just like in a very small way so  I learned a lot about that and i'm glad  i took the class mate yeah so as far as  my my expectations for the course one  thing I really realized taking this last  year is that this class and and design  and iterative design in general really  force you to learn skill sets that might  not be something that you come in with  so last year we worked on kind of a  robotic arm project I'm a mechanical  engineer and so some of the electronics  and the coding we're not really in my  wheelhouse if you will so this year I  was kind of the the mech e person of the  staff so what I was hoping was to be  able to help teams that needed a little  bit more mechanical design who maybe  come from backgrounds in electrical  engineering or computer signs and stuff  that might not have that skill set and I  think maybe we pulled that off would say  you have to hear from the the team's  about that one  so when we have a collaborate with our  client a lot of it was just figuring out  what would help her out the most because  um she came and told us immediately she  was like I have this disease and I have  bad grip because of it and so we have to  spend a long time thinking about okay  well what does that mean where it is  where is what does she what parts of her  hand does she gripped with what um when  does her grip slip why does it slip on  what can we do to sort of prevent it  from slipping what device could we even  possibly make and so a lot of the time  was just spent with her talking with her  about her experiences with her disease  and sort of figuring out very very  slowly what would actually help her and  then once we were able to get to that  point it sort of snowballed from there  we were like okay we actually know what  the problem is now so we can make this  device and then it was just build build  build and then we got it done network  um so for other on students or engineers  who want to collaborate with clients  with disabilities I think that it's a  really valuable experience and you  should definitely go for it I learned a  lot and was able to help someone and I  think that in itself made me really  happy um but in terms of things to keep  in mind uh the client comes first and  you have to always remember that you are  solving their problem not what you think  their problem is and we had to spend a  really long time actually getting to the  root of a problem which is why I think  in the end our product worked so  definitely listen to the client try to  get as much critical feedback from them  as possible even though sometimes that's  hard because they're just happy that  you're there helping them even if you're  not making them what you the best thing  that you possibly could and so  definitely listen to your client would  be the advice I would give  so I think the group work went really  well we were able to split it pretty  nicely into different sections of work  on in the beginning we were all pretty  much just talking with Beverly and a lot  about her disease and her problem but  once we were able to analyze the problem  we were able to sort of pick up niches  of the design that we were able to split  up pretty evenly and work on and then  combine in the end I think I definitely  figuring out how to split the work  evenly is the hardest part but once we  were able to do that it went really  smoothly  i would say for our team in particular  none of us had any circuitry experience  coming in and we were making something  we were making a device that was going  to stay on her at all times and it  definitely required a lot of circuitry  so the biggest problem was just figuring  out what kind of technology we even had  available to use and how we could use  that to our advantage learning about how  circuits worked even at like a very  basic level all that was just so foreign  to us and so that was definitely the  hardest part to get past  yeah so so as a 